# A function used as a VALUE, which is the one place where the two kinds of
# callee stop being interchangeable (design.md §4.13; spec § 13's callback
# sentence, *"a callback is a parameter, never a result"*).
#
# WHY IT IS ITS OWN FILE. It left `emit/inst.hero` at defect 069's repair, and
# not for length alone: naming a function is a question about the DECLARATION,
# while everything else in that file's dispatch is a question about the
# instruction's operands. The seam is the same one `emit/access.hero` sits on.
#
# WHAT THE ARM GOT WRONG, AND IT IS THE SHAPE CLAUDE.md § RUN IT WARNS ABOUT.
# Until 2026-09-20 the `extern_fn` case wrote `hero_unreachable(); /* the gate
# refuses this form */` and the emitter then went on to emit the CALL four lines
# later, passing a temporary nothing had assigned. So `check` was 0, `build` was
# 0 and the program aborted at run time saying *"this is a compiler bug, please
# report it"* — a true sentence pointing at the wrong line, for a form the
# checker admits and the spec's own §4.19 promises is a compile-time question.
# A gate that refuses a form and then emits it anyway is not a gate.
#
# WHY UNMANGLED (CLAUDE.md §7, and `emit/ops.hero`'s own `.extern_fn` arm says
# it for the call): the name in the `.hero` file IS the C function's name, and
# the `#include` is what declares it. Clang then checks the assignment against
# the real header's prototype — a mismatched function pointer is
# `incompatible function pointer types`, exit 1, which is the fifth
# clang-failure class of `.claude/rules/c-boundary.md` arriving at an
# assignment instead of at a call.

use ir
use ir/containers
use ir/instances
use source
use ast
use check/state
use emit/signature
use emit/writer

# The name a callee has when it stands as a value, or an error where the form
# has none. A Heroes function is named as a call names it, `instance` being
# the type arguments it is used at, empty for an ordinary one: a GENERIC
# function stands as a value at the instance its context named (defect 402),
# and the template, deleted by monomorphisation, has no C. `.builtin_fn` has
# no C designator at all — a built-in is a shape the emitter expands, not a
# symbol — and `.indirect` is already a pointer in a temporary, so its
# reference would be the address of an address, which `ir.hero`'s own comment
# refuses.
function designator(p: instances.Held, c: state.Checked, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], callee: ir.Callee, instance: [i64]) -> str?
    match callee
        .heroes_fn h =>
            found = instances.function_at(p, decl: h.decl, instance: instance)

            if found.is_err()
                return fail(code: "no_function", msg: "a Heroes function the program does not hold")
            return ok(signature.instance_name(p.program.functions[found.must()], c, s, decls))
        .extern_fn e  => return ok(source.span_text(s, decls[e.decl].name))
        .builtin_fn _ => return fail(code: "builtin", msg: "a built-in is expanded, never named")
        .indirect _   => return fail(code: "indirect", msg: "the address of an address")

# The assignment itself. A target the caller does not have means the value is
# discarded, and a function designator has no side effect, so nothing is
# written — the same reasoning `emit/unread.hero` applies everywhere else.
# `at` is the instruction's span end, where the type arguments are kept;
# `p` the program held with its index (defect 492).
function emit(@w: writer.Writer, p: instances.Held, f: containers.Function, c: state.Checked, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], callee: ir.Callee, at: i64, target: str?)
    if target.is_err()
        return
    named = designator(p, c, s, decls, callee: callee, instance: instances.named_at(f, c, at: at))

    if named.is_err()
        writer.line(@w, text: "    hero_unreachable(); /* a function value with no C name */")
        return
    writer.line(@w, text: "    " + target.must() + " = " + named.must() + ";")

## Tests

test "a Heroes function is mangled and an extern is not"
    assert designator_kind(.builtin_fn(at: 0)) == "none"
    assert designator_kind(.indirect(value: 0)) == "none"

# The two cases a unit test can reach without a parse: the two that have no
# name. The named cases are judged by the goldens, because the name comes from
# the source and a fabricated span would be the instrument watching itself.
function designator_kind(callee: ir.Callee) -> str
    match callee
        .heroes_fn _ | .extern_fn _ => return "named"
        .builtin_fn _ | .indirect _ => return "none"
